pr_comments:
I think the issue with `Vsync` should be related to the backend, and there might be some changes needed on the `Blade` side, but I'm not entirely sure. I tried a simple demo as below:
```rust
let mut msg = std::mem::zeroed();
        loop {
            if PeekMessageW(&mut msg, None, 0, 0, PM_REMOVE).as_bool() {
                if msg.message == WM_QUIT {
                    break;
                }
                TranslateMessage(&msg);
                DispatchMessageW(&msg);
            }
            InvalidateRect(raw.handle, None, FALSE);
        }
```
which just is a very basic loop, peeking message. When using wgpu as the backend and enabling Vsync, the program renders at 60fps, it halts when drawing until next vsync signaled, I guess this is what we want. However, when I tried similar code using Blade, the fps skyrocketed to around 3000.

Edit:
The wndproc func:
```rust
match msg {
        WM_DESTROY => PostQuitMessage(0),
        WM_PAINT => {
            inner.graphics.draw();
            inner.update_frame_rate();
        }
        _ => {}
    }
```

What really confuses me here is that `wgpu` actually achieves `vsync` by setting `vulkan::PresentMode::Fifo`, and `Blade` also sets the same parameter, but they show very different behaviors.
